================================================================
DATASET DECISION -- EMPIRICALLY VERIFIED 2026-09-08 (not paper claims)
================================================================
PICK: MCPCorpus (Lin, Ruan, Liu, Zhao -- ASE 2025 Tool Demonstrations,
arXiv:2506.23474). Verified by actually downloading and parsing it.

  Grab it with ONE curl, no token, no HuggingFace account:
    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Snakinya/MCPCorpus/main/Website/mcpso_servers_cleaned.json
    13 MB, 13,875 records, downloaded in 7.5 seconds.
  (HuggingFace mirror is blocked by the Claude sandbox egress proxy; GitHub is
   not. On the M2 Pro natively both should work. GitHub path needs no token.)

  Funnel on the real data (sample_corpus.py reproduces it):
    13,875 records
    -> 2,501 after filters (has repo, >=10 stars, not archived, not client)
    -> 2,222 UNIQUE repos after dedup (279 duplicate entries collapsed)
    -> 808 Python / 1,039 Node-TS / 375 other (other = no ToFu runner)
    -> 25 drawn, seed 20260908, fingerprint 053a84c90891
       (saved as targets_seed20260908_n25.csv)

  THREE REAL GOTCHAS FOUND BY TOUCHING THE DATA (all handled in the script):
   1. Do NOT filter on record["type"]=="server". That field is None on 12,976
      of 13,875 records; filtering FOR it silently discards 93% of the corpus.
      The servers file is servers-only by construction.
   2. Monorepos appear as MANY records sharing ONE repo --
      modelcontextprotocol/servers appears 35x, awslabs/mcp 7x, baidu-maps/mcp
      8x. Sampling without repo-level dedup reproduces exactly the flaw Pierre
      flagged (412 of 569 tools from one repo). Dedup step is mandatory.
   3. Only 82 repos in the filtered pool ship a tools schema in the corpus, so
      ToFu must clone and extract tool metadata itself (its Metadata Extraction
      stage already does this). Don't rely on the corpus 'tools' field.

  STALENESS -- state this honestly in the paper, it is a FEATURE not a bug:
  the MCPCorpus repo was last pushed 2025-09-01 and its records' last_commit
  dates are 2024-2025, so despite the paper's "evolvable" framing it is in
  practice a FIXED SNAPSHOT. That is BETTER for reproducibility: cite the
  MCPCorpus repo at a pinned commit and the population is frozen for any
  reader. If a fresher population is wanted, Crawler/ ships the collection
  scripts to re-run it (needs a GITHUB_TOKEN).

  Rejected as the primary corpus, with reasons:
   - Chord / Les Dissonances (NDSS 2026): its 66 targets are LangChain and
     LlamaIndex framework tools, NOT MCP servers. Wrong unit of analysis.
   - MCP at First Glance (TOSEM 2026) and Toeppe et al. (MSR 2026): both
     describe good, reproducible methodology and are stronger venues, but I
     could not verify a public downloadable artifact for either. Use them to
     JUSTIFY the >=10-star threshold (Hasan et al. use the same one) and as
     comparison points, not as the data source.

BASELINES RUNNABLE AGAINST THIS SAME CORPUS (for the comparison Pierre wants):
   - Cisco MCP Scanner  -- BEST BASELINE. Apache-2.0, Python, 1,068 stars,
     pushed 2026-09-08 (today). Install: uv tool install --python 3.13
     cisco-ai-mcp-scanner. Has a stdio mode (mcp-scanner stdio --stdio-command
     ...) so it can be driven in batch over a target list, and supports a LOCAL
     LLM so no API key/cost and no nondeterminism from a hosted model.
   - MCPSafetyScanner (johnhalloran321/mcpSafetyScanner) -- MPL-2.0, Python,
     178 stars, but STALE (last push 2025-04-10). This is the MCP Safety Audit
     paper's tool; already referenced (commented out) in table/motivation.tex.
   - MCPSecBench (AIS2Lab/MCPSecBench) -- MIT, Python, pushed 2026-03-04. Note
     it is an ATTACK benchmark, not a scanner, and its own ICLR reviewers said
     the harness is largely manual/GUI-driven -- so it is a weak automated
     baseline. Cite, do not lean on.
   - No public code found for VIPER-MCP, MCPZoo, or the academic MCP-Scanner
     (Abadeh et al.) as of this check.

  => Comparison design: run ToFu + Cisco MCP Scanner (+ MCPSafetyScanner if it
     still installs) over the SAME seeded 25-repo sample, then report what each
     finds and, importantly, what ToFu finds that the catalog-bounded scanners
     structurally cannot (the semantic/argument-level bugs). That is the
     experiment that substantiates the Table 2 claims.

