================================================================
SET D -- "IN VIPER-MCP'S COURT" (2026-09-10)
================================================================
Goal (your ask): pull VIPER-MCP's vs-Cisco-vs-MCPSafetyScanner comparison
straight from the VIPER-MCP paper and run ToFu against only those scenarios.

WHAT THE PAPER ACTUALLY GIVES (arXiv 2605.21392v2):
- Table 2 (RQ2): 260 servers = 130 vulnerable + 130 benign, all LLM parts on
  GPT-5.4. FNR on the vulnerable set:
     MCPSafetyScanner 63.8% | Cisco AI Defense 73.1% | VIPER-MCP 7.7%
  FPR on benign: 43.1% | 24.6% | 0%.
- VIPER models ONLY 3 classes: command injection, SSRF, path traversal.
  It EXPLICITLY does NOT model SQL injection or code injection (their own words
  for the residual 7.7% FN).
- Appendix B / Table 7: VIPER's 67 own 0-day servers with repo + class + lang.
- Table 1 + Appendix C: 7 case-study servers WITH the exact vulnerable tool
  and a verbatim PoC prompt.

THREE HARD LIMITATIONS (these bound what Set D can be -- do not paper over):
1. Table 2's 130+130 is NOT reproducible from the paper. The 63 historical
   public CVEs are never listed; only VIPER's own 67 discoveries are. So Set D
   can only be VIPER's own finds -> the deck is STACKED toward VIPER. Running
   ToFu here answers "can ToFu ALSO catch what VIPER caught", not a neutral
   head-to-head. Say this next to every Set D number.
2. Every CVE number in the arXiv PDF is redacted "CVE-2026-XXXXX", and NO
   commit hashes are published. The VIPER artifact (Viper-EA4D) explicitly
   REMOVED all datasets. So the vulnerable revision is unknown.
3. Because of (2), Set D commits are GitHub HEAD at resolve time. A repo
   patched since disclosure is a FALSE NEGATIVE THAT IS NOT TOFU'S FAULT.

WHAT WE BUILT:
- Benchmarks/extraction/set_d_manifest.json  -- ground truth transcribed from
  the paper (66 App.B rows -> 65 distinct repos; 54yyyu/code-mcp has 2 classes;
  + lanhu-mcp gold). Carries Table 2 numbers + all caveats. AUDIT THIS against
  the PDF.
- eval/resolve_set_d.py  -- resolves each repo to a live GitHub HEAD via
  git ls-remote (no API token). Re-runnable.
- Benchmarks/extraction/set_d.json  -- harness-ready, 63 repos resolved
  (6 gold w/ tool+PoC, 57 silver). Read by prepare_targets.py --only-set d.
- Benchmarks/extraction/set_d_unresolved.json -- the 3 we could NOT resolve.
- eval/score_set_d.py -- scores ToFu on Set D using VIPER's own FNR metric,
  with VIPER/Cisco/MCPSafetyScanner reference numbers.

HOW TO RUN (Set D is OPT-IN; NOT part of the default a+b sweep):
    python3 eval/resolve_set_d.py                 # refresh HEADs (optional)
    python3 eval/prepare_targets.py --only-set d  # clone+probe the 63 repos
    python3 eval/run_eval.py --time 600           # runs D configs too
    python3 eval/score_set_d.py --md set_d_scorecard.md

SCORING RULES baked into score_set_d.py (so results stay honest):
- A repo is a ToFu TP iff some tool fired a sanitizer mapping to the repo's
  ground-truth class (cmdi/ssrf/path). Mapping is in SANI_CLASS.
- Off-class finds (ToFu found a DIFFERENT bug) are reported separately -- NOT
  a detection, NOT ignored.
- TP via the LLM path vs via deterministic fallback are counted separately
  (a fallback TP is not evidence about ToFu's LLM design).
- Repos with only errored/no runs are NOT-RUN, excluded from the FNR
  denominator (never silently scored as misses).
- code_injection sanitizer is deliberately NOT mapped to cmdi -- VIPER excludes
  code injection, so crediting it would be unfair.

*** TO DOUBLE-CHECK (do not delete until you've looked) ***
