--- run_eval.py FINDING-DETECTION BUG (found + fixed 2026-09-10) ---
run_eval counted findings by strings ("sanitizer triggered", "violation",
"security finding", "oracle fired") that the reporter NEVER prints. The real
ConsoleVulnerabilityReporter (src/reporters.py) emits
  "[POTENTIAL VULNERABILITY / BUG FOUND] in '<tool>' !"
  "   -> Triggered Sanitizers : ['check_...']"
and fuzzer.py emits "Silent Bug Alert: Sanitizers [...] flagged ...". So even
AFTER the LLM fix, a genuine finding would have been recorded as finding_hits=0
-- the same silent-underreporting failure as the select_sanitizers bug.
FIXED: finding_hits now counts the real strings, and every run records
triggered_sanitizers=[...] (parsed from the log) so Set D can score by class.

================================================================
DEEP AUDIT OF THE EVAL HARNESS + LLM PATH -- 2026-09-10
(requested after the toolfuzzer -> ToFu rename; nothing was run)
================================================================

*** THE HEADLINE: "0 FINDINGS" WAS AN ARTIFACT. THE DATA WAS ON DISK. ***
eval/run_eval.py used re.finditer / re.search but never imported `re`. The
NameError fired at the parsing step -- AFTER the log had been written and
AFTER the subprocess had finished -- so the bare `except Exception` recorded
each run as ok=False, error="name 're' is not defined", finding_hits=0. Nine
completed runs were thrown away, not nine failed runs.

Re-parsing eval/runs/*.log (new `python3 eval/run_eval.py --reparse`) recovers
what the campaign actually produced:
    index said                          reality
    LLM selection ok on 2 runs   ->     ok on 7 of 9
    LLM seed-gen: not recorded   ->     ok on 6, fallback on 1
    0 findings                   ->     1390 raw alerts across 5 of 9 runs
                                        collapsing to 5 distinct signatures
                                        (exactly one per appium tool)
runs.jsonl has been rebuilt; the old index is at
eval/state/runs.jsonl.pre-reparse-20260910_032111.

*** BUT DO NOT REPORT THOSE 1390 AS FINDINGS. HERE IS WHY. ***
The "faulty inputs" in the logs are mojibake, e.g. '慬杮慵敧㨢∠慪慶Ⱒ'.
Decoded as UTF-16LE that is literally `language": "java", "useElement":` --
the JSON text of ToFu's OWN seed file. Another is `create_pdf": {"parameters":
{"content"` -- ToFu's own LLM tool-call schema. Root cause found:

  src/input_parser.py FuzzInputParser.parse() tried _generate_fallback_inputs
  FIRST. That method fills every declared property unconditionally, so it
  always returned a non-empty dict and the JSON branch below it was
  UNREACHABLE for any tool with a schema -- i.e. every MCP tool. A seed
  {"path": "../../../etc/passwd"} was therefore never parsed as JSON; its
  bytes went to atheris.FuzzedDataProvider and came back through
  ConsumeUnicodeNoSurrogates in a wide encoding.

  => The restored src/seed_bank/ and every LLM-generated seed have been acting
     as pure entropy. RQ2/RQ3's "semantic seeds drive the fuzzing" claim has
     had no mechanism behind it. The method being named "_generate_FALLBACK_
     inputs" while running first says the order was inverted at some point.

  FIXED: JSON-first, FDP-fallback, with seed values completing the schema.
  *** THIS CHANGES WHAT THE FUZZER SENDS. Read the new parse() docstring and
  sign off before the campaign -- it is the one audit change that alters
  measured behaviour rather than just recording it correctly. ***

*** SANITIZER SELECTION PRECISION -- first real evidence, and it is poor ***
Per-run, split into LLM-selected vs the always-on Layer-1 set
(sanitizers/__init__.py BASE_MANDATORY_SANITIZERS, 10 checks):
  generate-tap-code       n_selected=2  LLM-attributable fired: url_raw_input_validation
  generate-swipe-code     n_selected=4  url_{raw_input,hostname_canon,ssrf_ip_ranges,structure}
  generate-scroll-code    n_selected=3  markitdown_uri_validation
  generate-long-press     n_selected=2  markitdown_uri_validation
  generate-double-tap     n_selected=3  NONE -- 100% mandatory layer
llama3.1:8b picked check_markitdown_uri_validation (a document-conversion
check) and the SSRF/URL family for an Appium gesture CODE GENERATOR whose
schema is {language, useElement, elementId, x, y}. No URL, no document, no
file. This is the precision failure the previous session predicted, now
measured. It is a paper result -- and it means a stronger-model re-run is not
optional if RQ2/RQ3 are to say anything.

Also: 3 of the 4 sanitizers in most signatures (check_file_access,
check_process_and_fd_leaks, check_unhandled_promise_rejection) are MANDATORY,
so they fire regardless of the LLM. score_set_d.py splits TPs into "LLM path"
vs "deterministic fallback" but has no third bucket for "fired only because
Layer 1 is always on". Add it before Set D is scored.

- TO DOUBLE-CHECK (Ananto, do not erase until confirmed done):
    5. Sign off on the input_parser.py parse-order change before the campaign
       (it changes what the target receives).
    6. Decide whether check_file_access / check_process_and_fd_leaks firing on
       a pure string-templating tool is a true positive at all, or whether the
       Layer-1 tracers are catching the Node runtime's own I/O. If the latter,
       every mandatory-layer alert in the paper is noise and needs a filter.
    7. Whether to force-push the scrubbed history to PSecLab/toolfuzzer
       (see below) -- it rewrites every commit hash including Pierre's.

OTHER BUGS FOUND AND FIXED IN eval/run_eval.py
  - TIMEOUT DISCARDED EVERYTHING. subprocess.run(timeout=) raised, and the
    handler wrote no log and parsed nothing. With a 600s budget producing
    553-970s runs, timeouts were common, and each one threw away the whole
    log including any findings it had already produced. Now the output is
    captured, written and parsed on the timeout path too.
  - ORPHANED SERVERS. subprocess.run kills the direct child only, so each
    timed-out run leaked the node/python MCP server it had spawned. Now
    start_new_session=True + killpg.
  - HARNESS BUGS MASQUERADED AS TARGET FAILURES. One bare `except Exception`
    put our own NameError into the same `error` field a target crash uses.
    Now a separate `harness_error` field, counted and shouted about in the
    summary. This is what let the `re` bug hide for two campaigns.
  - finding_hits DOUBLE-COUNTED. It summed the reporter's banner AND the
    "Silent Bug Alert" text, which are two lines of ONE finding. Now the
    banner only, plus finding_classes = distinct sanitizer-set signatures
    (the number that belongs in the paper) and finding_signatures.
  - NO PROVENANCE. Records did not carry the time budget, model, endpoint,
    ToFu commit, python version or platform -- everything A3 (experimental
    setup) needs and none of it reconstructable later. Now recorded per run.
  - --set only accepted A|B, so Set D could not be run selectively despite
    the runbook. Now A|B|D.
  - NEW: --reparse rebuilds runs.jsonl from eval/runs/*.log. The log is now
    always written before it is parsed, so a parser bug can never destroy a
    campaign's data again.
  - NEW PREFLIGHT: run_eval now runs pyflakes over itself, llm.py, fuzzer.py
    and input_parser.py and REFUSES TO START if any undefined name exists.
    Both campaign-killing bugs this year were undefined names.

BUG: src/seed_generators.py WAS NEVER RESTORED
  Commit 87d8602 (Pierre, "latest edit") deleted src/seed_generators.py,
  src/interfaces.py, src/predefined_seeds/ (36 files), dictionaries/, configs/
  and 86 files total. The previous session restored src/seed_bank/ but missed
  seed_generators.py -- while src/llm.py still calls seed_generators.create_*
  in all 8 @tool functions. Every LLM seed-generation tool call died with
  "name 'seed_generators' is not defined" (visible in the 20:46 run records as
  "Error running tool create_json"). Restored src/seed_generators.py and all
  36 src/predefined_seeds/ files from b1b657e, and added the missing import.
  => the binary-seed path (create_pdf/docx/zip -> "predefined_seed:<name>")
     can now actually run. Whether it WORKS is untested; verify before the
     paper claims it.

CONCURRENCY BUG: ALL PARALLEL RUNS SHARED ONE CORPUS
  src/__main__.py never passed corpus_dir, so every run used the default
  ./fuzz_corpus relative to cwd=ROOT, and _setup_corpus_seeds() shutil.rmtree's
  that directory when it starts a tool. run_eval defaults to --workers 4, all
  with cwd=ROOT. So four concurrent runs were deleting and rewriting each
  other's corpora, while atheris read from a directory another process was
  removing. Every parallel campaign so far is non-reproducible for this reason
  alone. Added --corpus-dir and --metrics-dir to src/__main__.py; run_eval now
  gives each run eval/corpora/<config>/ and eval/metrics/<config>/.

RENAME FALLOUT (toolfuzzer -> ToFu), all repathed
  20 eval/configs/*.json, eval/state/prepared.jsonl, eval/README.md and 13
  .venv/bin console-script shebangs hardcoded /Users/.../pseclab/toolfuzzer.
  The configs are what feed the target launch command, so the campaign could
  not have started. ~2274 remaining hits inside .venv are RECORD/direct_url
  metadata and .pyc paths -- harmless. Historical logs under eval/runs/ and
  eval/state/quarantine-pre-llmfix/ keep the OLD path on purpose: they record
  what actually ran.
  Also cleared: a stale .git/index.lock from a crashed git process, which was
  making every `git checkout`/`git add` fail.

